      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ?$",
      "worked_solution": "The polar equation $r=2a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na=\\frac{(-3)}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ gives a circle of radius $|-1.5|=1.5$ centered at the point $(-1.5,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225098/assets/748c15bf1cb55a3ed5ce4d08c4458e12f6ad97df1f1658831968d41c8c8f4bbb.png)",
